      "min": 8,
      "duration": 9.0,
      "text": "Rendering lists with map.",
      "visual": "code",
      "items": [
        [
          "{todos.map(todo => (",
          "yellow"
        ],
        [
          "  <li key={todo.id}>",
          "blue"
        ],
        [
          "    {todo.text}",
          "green"
        ],
        [
          "  </li>",
          "blue"
        ],
        [
          "))}",
          "yellow"
        ]
      ],
      "say": "To render a list, map over your data and return an element for each item. Every item needs a key, and that key must identify the item.",
      "voice": "/audio/react-06-lists/map.wav"
    },
    {
      "id": "why",
      "min": 9,
      "duration": 14.5,
      "text": "Why keys matter.",
      "visual": "compare",
      "left": {
        "title": "Index as key",
        "color": "red",
        "items": [
          "Breaks on reorder",
          "State sticks to the wrong row"
        ]
      },
      "right": {
        "title": "Stable ID",
        "color": "green",
        "items": [
          "Tracks each item",
          "State follows the item"
        ]
      },
      "say": "React uses keys to match old items with new ones. With an index as the key, reordering or deleting items makes state, like a half-typed input, jump to the wrong row. A stable ID keeps each item's state with that item.",
      "voice": "/audio/react-06-lists/why.wav"
    },
    {
      "id": "rules",
      "min": 8,
      "duration": 11.0,
      "text": "The rules for keys.",
      "visual": "chips",
      "items": [
        [
          "Unique among siblings",
          "green"
        ],
        [
          "Stable between renders",
          "blue"
        ],
        [
          "Never Math.random()",
          "red"
        ],
        [
          "Not available as a prop",
          "yellow"
        ]
      ],
      "say": "Keys must be unique among siblings, and stable between renders, so never use a random number. They are for React's use, and don't arrive in the component as a prop.",
      "voice": "/audio/react-06-lists/rules.wav"
    },
    {
      "id": "conditional",
      "min": 9,
      "duration": 9.5,
      "text": "Conditional rendering.",
      "visual": "code",
      "items": [
        [
          "{isLoggedIn ? <Dashboard />",
          "green"
        ],
        [
          "            : <Login />}",
          "green"
        ],
        [
          "",
          "text"
        ],
        [
          "{hasError && <ErrorMessage />}",
          "blue"
        ],
        [
          "",
          "text"
        ],
        [
          "if (!user) return null;",
          "yellow"
        ]
      ],
      "say": "Use a ternary to pick between two outputs, the and operator to show something only when a condition holds, and an early return to render nothing at all.",
      "voice": "/audio/react-06-lists/conditional.wav"
    },
    {
      "id": "gotcha",
      "min": 8,
      "duration": 11.0,
      "text": "The stray zero.",
      "visual": "compare",
      "left": {
        "title": "count && <X />",
        "color": "red",
        "items": [
          "If count is 0",
          "it renders a literal 0"
        ]
      },
      "right": {
        "title": "count > 0 && <X />",
        "color": "green",
        "items": [
          "Always a boolean",
          "renders nothing, or X"
        ]
      },
      "say": "A well-known gotcha: using a number with the and operator. If the count is zero, React renders the zero. Compare it to a boolean first, so you get nothing instead.",
      "voice": "/audio/react-06-lists/gotcha.wav"
